ble bubble, which again is an unlikely coincidence. ### 3. The expansion would be anisotropic (different in different directions) for an off-center observer In a ball-shaped universe that is expanding, the expansion rate would appear faster in the direction toward the center (since the center is "more dense" or "more curved") and slower away from it. But we observe that the expansion is isotropic—the same in all directions—to high precision. This is exactly what you'd expect if every point is equivalent (no center), not if there's a special location. ### 4. The "solid ball" model has a fundamental problem with general relativity In the standard Big Bang theory, the universe **is not expanding into anything**—space itself is stretching. If the universe were a ball with a center, then the expansion would have to be like an explosion of matter from that center into empty space. But that would mean galaxies farther from the center would be moving faster, and we would see a clear "center" point where galaxies are moving slowly (or not at all). That's not what we see. Instead, every galaxy sees all others receding from it, with no galaxy at rest relative to the expansion. ### So why doesn't the "solid ball" idea work with the evidence? Ultimately, the universe as we observe it is **homogeneous** (the same density everywhere) and **isotropic** (looks the same in all directions). The only way a finite ball could look homogeneous and isotropic from every point inside it is if it's a **3-sphere** (the 3D surface of a 4D ball)—which is exactly the "finite but unbounded" model, like the balloon. In that model, there is no center *in the 3D space*; the center is in an extra dimension, just as the center of a balloon's surface is not on the surface. If the universe were a simple solid ball (with an inside and an edge), the homogeneity and isotropy would be broken. Since we observe homogeneity and isotropy to high precision, the solid ball is ruled out. In short: You're right that