cular way of visualizing the shape. You could embed the same 3-sphere in many different ways (with different centers), so the center is not an intrinsic property of the universe itself—it depends on how you choose to embed it. In general relativity, we describe the geometry of spacetime without needing an embedding; the universe stands on its own without any extra dimensions. So asking for the "center" in an embedding is like asking for the "up" direction in a coordinate system—it's a choice, not a fundamental truth. ### 3. What if the universe is not a 3-sphere? We don't actually know the exact shape of the universe. It could be infinite and flat (no center in any dimension), or it could be a finite shape without a simple spherical topology. Observations so far favor a flat, infinite (or very large) universe. In that case, even the mathematical embedding center disappears—there simply is no center at all, in any dimension. ### 4. So does that mean there is an "absolute center to find"? If by "find" you mean *locate within the physical universe*, then **no**—because the center, if it exists in a higher dimension, is not part of the physical universe. It's like saying "the center of a circle is a point on the line of the circle." That's a category mistake. The center is not on the circumference. If by "find" you mean *mathematically describe the geometry of the universe*, then we can say "the universe has a radius of curvature" (if it's a 3-sphere). But that radius is not a center point—it's a measure of curvature. The "center" is just a concept used in the embedding, not a physical location. ### The heart of it You're right that the balloon analogy *implies* a center in a higher space. But the key lesson of modern cosmology is that the universe does not need that extra space to exist. Reality is the 2D surface, not the full 3D balloon. So the question "Where is the center of the universe?" is answered by: **There is no center of the universe, because the universe ha